---
import RwLayout from '../../layouts/RwLayout.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;

const sections = [
  { slug: 'what-you-will-build', text: 'What you will build' },
  { slug: 'why-it-looks-the-way-it-does', text: 'Why it looks the way it does' },
  { slug: 'how-the-pages-are-organized', text: 'How the pages are organized' },
];
---

<RwLayout
  title="Reference Workflow — Scientific Agentic Engineering"
  description="A hands-on exercise in building scientific software with a coding agent, and in checking what it builds."
  current="overview"
  sections={sections}
>
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900">The Reference Workflow</h1>
    <p class="mt-3 text-lg text-gray-600">
      A hands-on exercise in building scientific software with a coding agent — and in checking
      what it builds.
    </p>

    <article class="prose prose-gray max-w-none mt-8">
      <h2 id="what-you-will-build">What you will build</h2>
      <p>
        A small forecast-verification application. It scores a week of recent forecasts from four
        weather models against the ERA5 reanalysis: it computes each model's error statistics by
        forecast lead and draws the comparison figures. The data is fetched once, at the start,
        and saved along with a record of where it came from.
      </p>
      <p>
        The exercise follows <a href={`${base}framework/the-loop/`}>the framework's workflow</a>.
        If you haven't read that chapter yet, it's worth reading first, since it explains why each
        step below is there. After a short setup, you work through four tasks in order: ingest,
        compute, visualize, and evaluate. Each task is one prompt to your coding agent, and each
        builds on the plan and files created by earlier tasks. The prompts are written for one
        continuous session, but those files are what carry the work forward, so a project you
        resume in a new session works just as well. In the three building tasks, the agent runs
        the workflow's stages: it writes a plan, renders it as a page you can open in your browser, and waits for your approval before
        implementing. Then it verifies its own work and waits for your review. The fourth task
        builds nothing new: the finished work goes to a fresh agent that had no part in building
        it (one your agent launches, or one you start yourself in a separate session), and the
        findings come back to you.
      </p>
      <p>
        The exercise practices one skill from end to end: working with an agent in a way that
        prevents or surfaces the
        <a href={`${base}framework/failure-modes/`}>documented failure modes</a> of AI-assisted
        scientific software, through to judging the fresh agent's findings yourself.
      </p>

      <h2 id="why-it-looks-the-way-it-does">Why it looks the way it does</h2>
      <p>
        The exercise is drawn from a real development episode: a forecast-comparison dashboard
        built with a coding agent in 2025, against the same data sources you are about to use. The
        failures that mattered in that episode were the quiet ones. A crash gets caught in the
        normal course of work; the runs that completed, rendered, and were wrong did not. Each
        task's page carries an episode note marking where that happened.
      </p>
      <p>
        The prompts behind those failures were ordinary feature requests, and today's coding
        agents might not make the same mistakes from them. The prompts here don't count on that.
        Each one names the checks that catch this kind of failure — unit conversions, time
        semantics, data wired to the wrong series — and has the agent show you its evidence, so a
        clean build is something you confirm rather than assume. What matters is understanding
        what each check guards against and why. Checks like these can also be packaged as skills
        that run them automatically, and that understanding is what lets you judge such a skill,
        or write one of your own.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each prompt states goals, constraints, and stopping points rather than step-by-step
        instructions, because that is the shape current frontier models work best from. Inside each one,
        the sections are marked with XML-style tags such as <code>&lt;goal&gt;</code> and
        <code>&lt;verify&gt;</code>, a structure agents parse reliably. You paste every prompt
        as it is. Where a choice is yours, such as the location to verify, the agent asks you.
      </p>

      <h2 id="how-the-pages-are-organized">How the pages are organized</h2>
      <p>
        Work through the chapters in order: setup, one chapter per task, and a short wrap-up on
        what you keep. Three appendices are there for reference as you go. The data primer covers
        what the APIs actually return, the visual checklist is the rubric for judging the figures,
        and the statistics page defines what gets computed. Each task links to the ones it uses.
      </p>
    </article>
</RwLayout>
